# 4. Your preservation section is actually pretty good — but I'd make one change

Your AST holding-time information is broadly supported by the Tasmanian Government laboratory's current published guidance. In particular, AST currently specifies:

- dissolved nutrients: filter and freeze ASAP, preferably within 24 hours; 28 days frozen
- total nutrients: freeze; 28 days frozen
- algae ID/count: Lugol's at 1% v/v; 6 months
- microbiological samples: refrigerate and 24 hours
- pH: 6 hours
- turbidity: 2 days
- EC: 28 days

:contentReference[oaicite:9]{index=9}

So that part of your resource has a **much stronger Tasmanian evidence base than I initially expected**.

However, you've written:

> "If you can't get samples to the lab on the same day, freeze them (for nutrients) within 24 hours."

I'd change that to:

> **Preservation and holding times depend on the parameter and laboratory method. Follow the laboratory's instructions for each analysis. For example, AST specifies different preservation and holding requirements for total nutrients, dissolved nutrients, microbiological samples and other analyses.**

That's safer because students shouldn't learn:

**"water samples = freeze if delayed."**

They need to learn:

**"preservation is analyte/method specific."**

AST itself recommends contacting the laboratory before sampling because container type, preservation, minimum volume and holding time depend on the analysis. :contentReference[oaicite:10]{index=10}

